/**
 * Unified entry point for all API services.
 *
 * <p>Takes an {@link Authenticator} (one credential for every operation) or, through
 * {@link #builder(String)}, one optional credential per security scheme named after the
 * scheme, and optionally {@link TransportOptions}. It then exposes each API group as a typed
 * property. If an authenticator implements {@link HttpAwareAuthenticator}, the shared
 * {@link ApiClient} is injected so that authentication HTTP calls (token exchange, discovery)
 * use the same transport configuration as regular API calls.
 *
 * <p>Each operation uses the credential of the first scheme in its own security list that the
 * client holds, falls back to the single authenticator when it holds none of them, and sends
 * no credential when there is neither. There is no per-call scheme override.
 *
 * <p>Usage:
 * <pre>{@code
 * // One credential for every operation
 * {{clientClassName}} client = new {{clientClassName}}(authenticator);
 *
 * // Custom transport (proxy, timeouts, etc.)
 * TransportOptions transport = TransportOptions.builder()
 *     .proxy("http://proxy:3128")
 *     .timeout(5000)
 *     .build();
 * {{clientClassName}} client = new {{clientClassName}}(authenticator, transport);
{{#hasClientSchemes}}
 *
 * // One optional credential per security scheme, named after the scheme
 * {{clientClassName}} client = {{clientClassName}}.builder(host)
{{#clientSchemes}}
 *     .{{methodName}}(...)
{{/clientSchemes}}
 *     .transportOptions(transport)
 *     .build();
{{/hasClientSchemes}}
 * }</pre>
 *
 * <p>The client is {@link AutoCloseable}, and {@link #close()} is graceful and idempotent: it
 * marks the client closed and returns, requests already in flight finish, and the HTTP stack
 * the SDK built itself is released when the last of them completes. It never closes a
 * {@link java.net.http.HttpClient} the caller supplied through {@link TransportOptions}. A
 * request issued after closing fails with an {@link IllegalStateException}.
 */
